29
00:03:11,022 --> 00:03:13,540
That's why we call them large language models, right?

30
00:03:13,838 --> 00:03:16,640
The first idea is something called reverse indexing.

31
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:18,372
Now, everybody knows about this, right?

32
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,320
It's the back to the book index.

33
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,160
That's where search engines come from.

34
00:03:22,414 --> 00:03:26,000
This is where Jeff Dean made his name, right?

35
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:32,420
MapReduce, big table, all this technology, just to be able to build a giant index of the entire web.

36
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:33,060
right?

37
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:34,560
This is where search engine came from.

38
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:42,404
The second idea, if you have never taken a computer science class, that's probably the most important, is the idea of vector spaces.

39
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:48,240
And using vector spaces to model language and to model various different kinds of things.

40
00:03:48,526 --> 00:03:49,760
The idea here is very simple.

41
00:03:49,920 --> 00:04:01,924
If you have a document, you have lots of words in it, you just count up the frequency of those words and use relationship between those vectors, those frequencies, to get a sense of the similarity between documents.

42
00:04:02,638 --> 00:04:13,956
Now, I want to explain to you that the first mistake I made as a graduate student many years ago was my PhD advisor, John Riedel, who came to me and said, I got an idea.

43
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:18,820
We're going to turn documents into users and words into items.

44
00:04:19,406 --> 00:04:24,036
And we're going to use this matrix to describe the relationship between users and items.

45
00:04:24,558 --> 00:04:29,028
And we'll use all the techniques from information retrieval to build recommendation engines.

46
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:32,708
Cosine similarity, blah, blah, blah, all that stuff.

47
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:35,600
I said, John, that was a stupid idea.

48
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:40,228
I've already heard about this idea, Pearson correlation, that was invented in the 1900s.

49
00:04:40,814 --> 00:04:43,280
I mean, who cares about these ideas at this point?

50
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:48,000
Well, as you know, recommendation technology at this point behind what?

51
