When I told you I was a slow learner, the first time I read the abstract, I was like, shit, I didn't even understand the first phrase.

129
00:09:53,038 --> 00:09:56,516
Can anyone explain to me what the word transduction means?

130
00:09:57,742 --> 00:10:05,184
If you have listened to this talk before, or longer, much longer version, there's an hour-long version of this, you're disqualified from answering.

131
00:10:05,344 --> 00:10:08,452
But has anyone, does anyone know the word transduction?

132
00:10:10,094 --> 00:10:10,304
Okay.

133
00:10:10,958 --> 00:10:12,004
Yeah, what does it mean?

134
00:10:19,904 --> 00:10:24,944
Now this is interesting because uh essentially sequence in and sequence out.

135
00:10:25,024 --> 00:10:31,264
But the original term, transduction, means to change one energy wave into another energy wave.

136
00:10:31,424 --> 00:10:44,304
By the way, some of you invest in biology, in biology sequences, which was the first research area that I was in, where you take DNA sequences and turn it into protein sequences, a process called transcription.

137
00:10:44,384 --> 00:10:49,184
That's also called transduction, because it's a little DNA sequence into protein sequences.

138
00:10:49,454 --> 00:10:53,092
So it's sequence transduction, that's the world's first sequence transduction.

139
00:10:53,504 --> 00:10:54,660
God invented it.

140
00:10:55,184 --> 00:10:55,504
Okay.

141
00:10:56,334 --> 00:11:02,692
Now, what's interesting is that sequence transduction is happening right now, and you didn't even realize it.

142
00:11:03,598 --> 00:11:13,252
A microphone is a transductor because it's a sequence of sound waves that turns into an electrical wave and goes into your ear.

143
00:11:13,678 --> 00:11:21,444
And by the way, your ear is also a transductor because there's an eardrum that vibrates according to the sound that you're hearing.

144
00:11:21,966 --> 00:11:29,284
And here's the magical part that sound turns into neural activities in your brain, and that is why we're able to communicate.

145
00:11:30,446 --> 00:11:38,468
I have thoughts in my brain that goes into my vocal cords, which turns into sound, which turns into neural activities in your brain.

146
00:11:38,990 --> 00:11:47,524
And then your brain interprets those neural activities, a sequence of neural activities, into concepts, and that's why we're able to communicate.

147
00:11:48,014 --> 00:11:50,020
This is the beauty of human communication.

148
00:11:50,766 --> 00:11:53,828
Without sequence transduction, it doesn't happen.

149
00:11:54,990 --> 00:11:56,132
You might as well go to sleep.

150
00:11:57,038 --> 00:12:03,332
Literally, if it doesn't happen, you might well have slept in this morning, right, instead of coming here.

151
00:12:04,078 --> 00:12:14,788
So the beauty of the world that we're living in now in AI is that we figured out a way to mimic that sequence transduction process.

152
00:12:15,790 --> 00:12:23,584
The amazing thing about this, by the way, a lot of people don't know this, the first paper that introduced this term was published in 2013.

153
00:12:23,694 --> 00:12:25,924
And that paper never won an award.

154
00:12:26,222 --> 00:12:28,260
It's written by a guy named Alex Graves.

155
00:12:29,102 --> 00:12:38,020
Now, what is amazing about this is that it turns out that these ideas was the basis for something called convergence.

156
00:12:38,704 --> 00:12:39,332
Why?

157
00:12:39,758 --> 00:12:47,652
Because it turns out what you can do is you can compress all these neural capabilities into a single model.

158
00:12:48,206 --> 00:12:58,084
When Google first started doing machine translation, 40 to 40 other languages, that would have required 1,560 different models.

159
00:12:59,886 --> 00:13:04,452
But by building this one model, we can do translation across all the different languages.

160
00:13:04,878 --> 00:13:09,316
By the way, including Klingon, for those of you who are Star Trek fans.

161
00:13:11,054 --> 00:13:14,180
Now, it turns out that sequence transduction was not enough.

162
00:13:15,022 --> 00:13:16,004
Do you know why it's not enough?

163
00:13:16,910 --> 00:13:38,436
Because your brain, right now, as I mentioned, is already doing sequence transduction, because it's already completing my words, and your brain is doing word prediction.

164
00:13:39,790 --> 00:13:41,764
But you don't know what I'm thinking next, do you?

165
00:13:43,598 --> 00:13:45,444
You have no idea what I'm going to say next.

166
00:13:46,030 --> 00:13:47,172
That's why you're here.

167
00:13:47,438 --> 00:13:52,292
Because if you can predict everything I'm going to say in the next five minutes, you wouldn't be sitting here.

168
00:13:54,798 --> 00:13:55,524
Think about that.

169
00:13:56,078 --> 00:14:01,508
So that is why sequence transduction or next work prediction is not enough.

170
00:14:02,222 --> 00:14:11,652
So in 2021, a friend of mine who later became one of my reports, Danny Zoe, came to me and said, I want to work on reasoning.

171
00:14:11,886 --> 00:14:13,188
And I said, I agree.

172
00:14:13,742 --> 00:14:19,716
I had all this training in cognitive science, and I said, why can't we teach machines the way we teach kids?

173
00:14:20,558 --> 00:14:23,716
So the idea came to us, the two of us.

174
00:14:24,270 --> 00:14:33,696
The idea was instead of just doing input and output like we do in machine learning, maybe we should insert explanations as to why the input maps to the output.

175
00:14:33,776 --> 00:14:36,196
So for example, you got a math problem.

176
00:14:37,134 --> 00:14:38,084
Imagine this.

177
00:14:38,478 --> 00:14:46,496
In 2021, when we tried it on GPT, simple math problem like this, with success rate was lower than 30%.

178
00:14:47,246 --> 00:14:59,172
After we used chain of thought by inserting that explanation as part of the token transduction, the performance went up by 2x, sometimes 4x.

179
00:15:00,398 --> 00:15:03,012
That was the beginning of reasoning machines.

180
00:15:03,536 --> 00:15:08,228
That was, in my personal opinion, the beginning of real intelligence.

181
00:15:08,910 --> 00:15:09,136
Okay.

182
00:15:09,646 --> 00:15:18,788
So what happened was we went from next token prediction to next idea prediction.

183
00:15:20,046 --> 00:15:23,556
And that is key to reasoning.

184
00:15:23,918 --> 00:15:25,348
That is key to planning.

185
00:15:25,646 --> 00:15:27,748
That is the key to thought processes.

186
00:15:28,110 --> 00:15:29,924
That's why we call it chain of thought.

187
00:15:31,022 --> 00:15:34,276
Chain of thought is for planning, it's for thinking, it's for reasoning.

188
00:15:35,630 --> 00:15:40,420
Now, in retrospect, post-training with chain of thought was the beginning of the large reasoning.

189
00:15:41,136 --> 00:15:45,124
And these are the three ideas that really matter during that 10 period time.

190
00:15:45,616 --> 00:15:51,844
Now, if you are a scientist, you're an investor, what you really care about is what are the next big ideas?

