00:45:27,406 --> 00:45:35,140
That's a great question because a lot of people didn't entirely understand what was really going on with chain of thought reasoning.

475
00:45:35,438 --> 00:45:50,944
The idea was, as I explained in my talk, that if the problem of thinking is about generating tokens one by one in a sequential way, then the question was that maybe the model already contains that chain of thought.

476
00:45:51,024 --> 00:45:52,464
That is the correct answer.

477
00:45:52,654 --> 00:45:56,464
We just had to dig it, you know, dig into it to find it.

478
00:45:56,718 --> 00:46:03,584
So go from passive 16 to passive four to passive one for those of you who are engineers who are thinking about this problem.

479
00:46:03,744 --> 00:46:05,104
So you just had to dig it out.

480
00:46:05,326 --> 00:46:07,184
The question is, how are you going to dig it out?

481
00:46:07,264 --> 00:46:12,996
So that it's the most intelligent thing that you could have said, at least when it comes to solving a problem.

482
00:46:13,744 --> 00:46:23,876
And it turns out that a lot of people didn't understand this, which is that if you thought about next token prediction, for those of you who have learned computer science, this is a Turing machine.

483
00:46:24,464 --> 00:46:31,904
A Turing machine was something that reads a tape, that reads symbols, and then generates symbols in a sequential way.

484
00:46:32,064 --> 00:46:39,364
So it turned out that transformers and sequence learning models were just neural architectures of Turing machines.

485
00:46:40,384 --> 00:46:47,268
So once you viewed it from that perspective, all you had to do was tune the Turing machine so it generates the correct sequence.

486
00:46:47,984 --> 00:46:49,828
That was the key insight.

487
00:46:50,144 --> 00:46:51,904
Now, what was the key insight?

488
00:46:51,984 --> 00:47:03,504
Actually, was that chain of thought in context learning, as Josh was just talking about, was that what people are calling as context buffer is really a program.

489
00:47:04,878 --> 00:47:10,148
Let me pause that for a real second just so that you really ingrain that idea.

490
00:47:10,702 --> 00:47:22,692
The idea is that if you put tokens in the context buffer, what you're doing is you're programming the neural Turing machine that changes how it what symbol is going to generate next.

491
00:47:23,790 --> 00:47:31,844
So if I put in a program that says, here's how you solve the problem, it's gonna follow that program to solve it correctly.

492
00:47:32,398 --> 00:47:34,820
That was the key insight from the chain of thought paper.

493
00:47:35,886 --> 00:47:40,260
That's how you dig out the correct intelligence, and that's how you teach your kids.

494
00:47:40,782 --> 00:47:43,268
You say, don't solve the problem that way, solve it this way.

495
00:47:44,110 --> 00:47:44,836
It's the same thing.

